      <!-- Worked Step-by-Step Laboratory Example -->
      <h3 style="font-size: 1.35rem; font-weight: 800; color: #0f172a; margin-top: 2.5rem; margin-bottom: 0.75rem;">
        4. Step-by-Step Worked Example: Hydrocarbon Combustion
      </h3>
      <p>
        Consider the complete combustion of propane gas (C<sub>3</sub>H<sub>8</sub>) reacting with molecular oxygen (O<sub>2</sub>):
      </p>
      <div style="background: #f8fafc; border: 1.5px solid #cbd5e1; border-radius: 8px; padding: 1.25rem; margin: 1.25rem 0;">
        <p style="margin: 0 0 0.5rem; font-weight: 700; color: #0f172a;">Problem Statement:</p>
        <p style="margin: 0 0 0.75rem; font-size: 0.92rem; color: #475569;">
          A student combusts 22.05 g of propane (C<sub>3</sub>H<sub>8</sub>, M<sub>w</sub> = 44.097 g/mol) in the presence of 64.00 g of oxygen (O<sub>2</sub>, M<sub>w</sub> = 31.998 g/mol). Determine the limiting reactant and theoretical yield of carbon dioxide (CO<sub>2</sub>, M<sub>w</sub> = 44.009 g/mol).
        </p>
        
        <p style="margin: 0 0 0.5rem; font-weight: 700; color: #0f172a;">Step 1: Balance the Chemical Equation</p>
        <p style="margin: 0 0 0.75rem; font-size: 0.92rem; color: #475569; font-family: monospace; font-weight: bold;">
          C<sub>3</sub>H<sub>8</sub> + 5 O<sub>2</sub> &rarr; 3 CO<sub>2</sub> + 4 H<sub>2</sub>O
        </p>

        <p style="margin: 0 0 0.5rem; font-weight: 700; color: #0f172a;">Step 2: Convert Reagent Masses to Moles</p>
        <p style="margin: 0 0 0.75rem; font-size: 0.92rem; color: #475569;">
          n(C<sub>3</sub>H<sub>8</sub>) = 22.05 g / 44.097 g/mol = 0.500 mol<br />
          n(O<sub>2</sub>) = 64.00 g / 31.998 g/mol = 2.000 mol
        </p>

        <p style="margin: 0 0 0.5rem; font-weight: 700; color: #0f172a;">Step 3: Evaluate Reaction Extent to Identify the Limiting Reactant</p>
        <p style="margin: 0 0 0.75rem; font-size: 0.92rem; color: #475569;">
          &xi;(C<sub>3</sub>H<sub>8</sub>) = 0.500 mol / 1 = 0.500 mol<br />
          &xi;(O<sub>2</sub>) = 2.000 mol / 5 = 0.400 mol<br />
          Because &xi;(O<sub>2</sub>) &lt; &xi;(C<sub>3</sub>H<sub>8</sub>), <strong>molecular oxygen (O<sub>2</sub>) is the limiting reactant</strong>.
        </p>

        <p style="margin: 0 0 0.5rem; font-weight: 700; color: #0f172a;">Step 4: Calculate Theoretical Mass of Formed Product</p>
        <p style="margin: 0; font-size: 0.92rem; color: #475569;">
          n(CO<sub>2</sub>) = &xi;<sub>min</sub> &times; &nu;(CO<sub>2</sub>) = 0.400 mol &times; 3 = 1.200 mol<br />
          m<sub>theoretical</sub>(CO<sub>2</sub>) = 1.200 mol &times; 44.009 g/mol = 52.81 g
        </p>
      </div>

