      <!-- Common Laboratory Pitfalls -->
      <h3 style="font-size: 1.35rem; font-weight: 800; color: #0f172a; margin-top: 2.5rem; margin-bottom: 0.75rem;">
        6. Critical Benchtop Pitfalls &amp; Yield Discrepancies
      </h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Competing Side Reactions:</strong> In incomplete combustions or organometallic syntheses, secondary pathways consume limiting reactants without generating the desired product.</li>
        <li><strong>Crystalline Hydration States:</strong> Neglecting bound water (e.g., weighing CuSO<sub>4</sub>&middot;5H<sub>2</sub>O as anhydrous CuSO<sub>4</sub>) results in severely deficient active reagent addition.</li>
        <li><strong>Percent Yield Exceeding 100%:</strong> In synthetic crystallization, an apparent yield over 100% indicates solvent entrapment, unevaporated mother liquor, or salt co-precipitation.</li>
      </ul>

      <!-- FAQs Accordion -->
      <h3 style="font-size: 1.5rem; font-weight: 800; color: #0f172a; margin-top: 3rem; margin-bottom: 1.25rem;">
        Frequently Asked Questions: Reaction Stoichiometry
      </h3>

      <div style="display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 0.85rem; margin-bottom: 2rem;">
        <details style="background: #ffffff; border: 1.5px solid #cbd5e1; border-radius: 10px; padding: 1.15rem;">
          <summary style="font-weight: 800; color: #0f172a; cursor: pointer; font-size: 0.95rem;">How does the chemical equation solver and formula balancer work?</summary>
          <p style="margin: 0.65rem 0 0 0; font-size: 0.9rem; color: #334155; line-height: 1.7;">
            The chemical equation solver constructs a stoichiometric matrix where rows represent unique elements and columns represent chemical species. By performing Gaussian elimination using exact rational arithmetic, it solves the homogeneous system to yield the smallest positive integer nullspace vector for chem balancing equations.
          </p>
        </details>

        <details style="background: #ffffff; border: 1.5px solid #cbd5e1; border-radius: 10px; padding: 1.15rem;">
          <summary style="font-weight: 800; color: #0f172a; cursor: pointer; font-size: 0.95rem;">How do you solve stoichiometry igcse and basic stoichiometry problems?</summary>
          <p style="margin: 0.65rem 0 0 0; font-size: 0.9rem; color: #334155; line-height: 1.7;">
            Stoichiometry igcse and basic stoichiometry problems are solved by following three core steps: (1) write and balance the chemical equation, (2) convert given masses to moles using molar mass, and (3) apply stoichiometric molar ratios to find the unknown quantity or limiting reagent.
          </p>
        </details>

        <details style="background: #ffffff; border: 1.5px solid #cbd5e1; border-radius: 10px; padding: 1.15rem;">
          <summary style="font-weight: 800; color: #0f172a; cursor: pointer; font-size: 0.95rem;">What is the difference between gravimetric stoichiometry and solution stoichiometry?</summary>
          <p style="margin: 0.65rem 0 0 0; font-size: 0.9rem; color: #334155; line-height: 1.7;">
            Gravimetric stoichiometry involves solid reactant masses and precipitate isolation (such as in precipitation reactions), whereas solution stoichiometry utilizes liquid volumes and molarity concentrations (such as in acid base titration stoichiometry).
          </p>
        </details>

        <details style="background: #ffffff; border: 1.5px solid #cbd5e1; border-radius: 10px; padding: 1.15rem;">
          <summary style="font-weight: 800; color: #0f172a; cursor: pointer; font-size: 0.95rem;">How do you determine the limiting reactant in combustion stoichiometry?</summary>
          <p style="margin: 0.65rem 0 0 0; font-size: 0.9rem; color: #334155; line-height: 1.7;">
            In combustion stoichiometry (e.g., burning hydrocarbons with oxygen), you calculate the reaction extent (xi = moles / coefficient) for each reactant. The reagent with the smaller extent is completely consumed and limits the theoretical yield of carbon dioxide and water.
          </p>
        </details>

        <details style="background: #ffffff; border: 1.5px solid #cbd5e1; border-radius: 10px; padding: 1.15rem;">
          <summary style="font-weight: 800; color: #0f172a; cursor: pointer; font-size: 0.95rem;">Why can an experimental percent yield exceed 100%?</summary>
          <p style="margin: 0.65rem 0 0 0; font-size: 0.9rem; color: #334155; line-height: 1.7;">
            A percent yield above 100% indicates product contamination. Common sources include residual crystallization solvents, unevaporated water, co-precipitated byproduct salts, or unreacted starting materials in the isolated product.
          </p>
        </details>

        <details style="background: #ffffff; border: 1.5px solid #cbd5e1; border-radius: 10px; padding: 1.15rem;">
          <summary style="font-weight: 800; color: #0f172a; cursor: pointer; font-size: 0.95rem;">Where can I find stoichiometry examples with solutions?</summary>
